    ],
    "exampleHtml": "There are <span class=\"vp-verb\">many</span> chairs, but there isn't <span class=\"vp-prep\">much</span> space. — I've got <span class=\"vp-verb\">a few</span> apples and <span class=\"vp-prep\">a little</span> milk.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>Countable</strong> nouns can have a number: <em>one book, two books, a chair, three friends.</em> They have a plural and take <em>are</em>. <strong>Uncountable</strong> nouns have no plural and no <em>a / an</em>: <em>water, milk, money, time, bread, music, homework.</em> They take <em>is</em>.",
        "tag": "Countable or uncountable?"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>many</strong> + plural countable noun: <em>many books, many people.</em> <strong>much</strong> + uncountable noun: <em>much water, much time.</em> We use them mostly in <strong>negatives and questions</strong>: <em>I haven't got many friends. Have you got much time?</em>",
        "tag": "many and much"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>a lot of</strong> (or <strong>lots of</strong>) works with both kinds of noun: <em>a lot of books, a lot of water.</em> In <strong>positive sentences</strong> use it: <em>We've got a lot of homework. There are a lot of people here.</em>",
        "tag": "a lot of"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>a few</strong> + plural countable noun: <em>a few apples.</em> <strong>a little</strong> + uncountable noun: <em>a little milk.</em> Both mean a small amount, and that is enough: <em>I've got a few friends here.</em> (= some)",
        "tag": "a few and a little"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>How many</strong> + plural countable noun: <em>How many people are there?</em> <strong>How much</strong> + uncountable noun: <em>How much milk do you need?</em> <em>How much</em> also asks for a price: <em>How much is this jacket?</em> After <em>How</em> we do not use <em>a lot of</em>.",
        "tag": "How many? How much?"
      },
      {
        "html": "✗ <em>How much people are there?</em> → ✓ <em>How many people are there?</em> · ✗ <em>I have many homework.</em> → ✓ <em>I have a lot of homework.</em> · ✗ <em>two breads, an information</em> → ✓ <em>two loaves of bread, a piece of information</em> · ✗ <em>a few water</em> → ✓ <em>a little water</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "<em>few</em> and <em>little</em> without <em>a</em> mean \"not enough\": <em>I've got few friends here</em> sounds sad. <em>a few</em> and <em>a little</em> are positive. Some uncountable nouns are countable in other languages, for example <em>advice, information, news, furniture, luggage</em>. Count them with <em>a piece of</em>: <em>a piece of advice.</em> <em>Some</em> and <em>any</em> also work with both kinds of noun, see <a href=\"/some-any\">some and any</a>. Revise the plural at <a href=\"/plurals\">Plural nouns</a> and <em>a / an</em> at <a href=\"/articles\">Articles</a>.",
